<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Preserving Our Heritage</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning How We Take Care of Our Past</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain general concepts in simple terms. Text: Summarize information from instructions or announcements.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain the importance of heritage sites to classmates, using simple language and a map or image of a heritage site to emphasize cultural value (e.g., shows a timeline of restoration efforts and explains, "Casco Viejo is important because it represents Panama's colonial past and restoration efforts.")
            Can summarize instructions for a field trip to a historical site, ensuring classmates understand the objectives by using visual aids (e.g., shows a timeline of events and says, "We'll learn about Panama's history and see how it connects to today.")
            Can clarify the purpose of cultural preservation by using bilingual explanations and visuals in peer discussions (e.g., "Preserving our heritage sites helps us remember and share our history," with a translated summary if needed).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various Panamanian heritage sites (e.g., Casco Viejo, Panama Viejo ruins, Emberá villages, the Panama Canal). Students briefly discuss in pairs: "Which of these sites have you visited or heard about? Why are these places important to our identity?" (3-4 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain the importance of a site in simple terms and summarize information. The teacher shows an image of Casco Viejo and says: "Casco Viejo, which is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is important because it represents Panama's colonial past and shows restoration efforts. It attracts tourists from around the world. We will continue to preserve its legacy." The teacher also models the pronunciation of /eɪ/ in "trade," "became," and "great," and the reduced vowel in "important" (/ɪmˈpɔː.tənt/) and "heritage" (/ˈher.ɪ.tɪdʒ/). (3-4 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I need to explain something difficult to a friend, what skill am I using?" (Expected answer: Mediation/explaining/simplifying).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I tell you only the main points of a long story, what am I doing?" (Expected answer: Summarizing).</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher provides examples of simple explanations and summaries, checking for comprehension. (2-3 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8-11 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes a mock "Project Brief" for the "Cultural Heritage Website" (genre: instructions) and a short "Announcement for a Local Heritage Festival" (genre: announcement). These texts contain specific instructions and details about heritage preservation, using target grammar (relative clauses, past simple, present perfect) and vocabulary (heritage, sites, restoration, legacy, identity).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpreting and expressing information from various sources effectively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the texts individually.
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (Website Project Brief):</b> "What is the main goal of the Cultural Heritage Website project?" "What specific information must be included for each site?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (Festival Announcement):</b> Students match festival activities (e.g., traditional weaving, historical reenactments) to their corresponding times or locations, which are described using relative clauses (e.g., "The main stage, where traditional weaving demonstrations will take place, offers...") or past simple ("The festival opened last year...").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time: 7-9 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Explaining general concepts in simple terms; summarizing instructions; using relative clauses ("which," "where"), past simple vs. present perfect, and future simple for predictions. Vocabulary: heritage, sites, restoration, legacy, identity, preserve, attract, became, transformed, continue, relevant, historical, cultural, significant.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Concept Clarification):</b> The teacher provides a list of complex sentences or concepts related to heritage (e.g., "The economic impact of cultural tourism involves a multifaceted analysis of revenue streams and local infrastructure development.") Students work individually to rewrite or explain these concepts in simple terms, using vocabulary like 'tourists', 'economy', 'money', 'local'. They then share their simplified versions in small groups. (4-5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Instruction Summarization):</b> In pairs, students receive short paragraphs from the "Project Brief" or "Festival Announcement." They must summarize these paragraphs using a maximum of two sentences, focusing on the main idea. They should include at least one relative clause ("This section, which outlines the project requirements...") or contrast past simple/present perfect ("The site opened in 1914, and it has attracted many tourists since then."). (5-6 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Role-play Explanation):</b> Students role-play a scenario where one student is an expert on "Preserving Our Heritage" and the other is someone who knows little. The "expert" explains the importance of cultural preservation (e.g., "Why is preserving historic landmarks like Casco Viejo important?") using simple language and future simple for predictions ("We will continue to preserve our cultural and historical heritage because..."). The "learner" asks clarifying questions. (5-6 min)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time: 14-17 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Mini-Presentation / Explanation (directly linked to the Cultural Heritage Website project).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in their "Cultural Heritage Website" project groups (established in earlier lessons). Each group selects one key historical site featured on their website. Their task is to act as tour guides or educators, mediating information about this site to their classmates (who are role-playing as interested visitors or younger students). They prepare a 2-3 minute presentation where they must:
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Explain the importance of the site:</b> Use simple language and visuals from their website (or a map/image) to emphasize its cultural value. (e.g., "Casco Viejo is important because it represents Panama's colonial past and restoration efforts.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarize key historical facts:</b> Use past simple vs. present perfect (e.g., "The Panama Canal opened in 1914," "It has attracted many tourists").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarify its significance:</b> Use relative clauses (e.g., "This is the section where we explain the site's legacy," or "The traditional villages, which offer unique cultural experiences, are also highlighted.") and future simple for predictions regarding its preservation ("We will continue to preserve this landmark.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourage a respectful and engaging tone, showing interest in cultural diversity.</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher circulates, providing support and noting language use. (10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10-12 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and Peer Assessment. During the presentations, students use a simple peer assessment rubric to evaluate their classmates' ability to mediate information. The teacher uses an observation checklist focusing on the learning outcomes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group's mini-presentation/explanation from Stage 4 serves as the assessment task. They will present a 2-3 minute mediated explanation of their chosen heritage site from their "Cultural Heritage Website."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer/Teacher):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and simplicity of explanation (ability to explain general concepts).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy and completeness of summary (summarizing instructions/information).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of relative clauses ("which," "where"), past simple vs. present perfect, and future simple.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of vocabulary (heritage, sites, restoration, legacy, identity, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of target sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Respectful tone and engagement (sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of visual aids (from their website).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining/summarizing information about the heritage site did I do well today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one challenge I faced when trying to simplify complex ideas for my classmates, and how might I improve next time?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time: 7-9 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How important is the skill of mediation (explaining simply, summarizing) when sharing our cultural heritage with others, especially those who might not know much about it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategies did I use today to make my explanations clearer or my summaries more concise?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of the mediation tasks worked particularly well? Did students effectively use relative clauses and the past simple/present perfect contrast to clarify information? Were they able to simplify complex concepts related to heritage? What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed in future lessons to further develop their mediation skills or support the "Cultural Heritage Website" project, especially concerning bilingual explanations if needed?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Preserving Our Heritage" theme. The next theme will build on students' ability to synthesize and present information, possibly focusing on cross-cultural communication or global issues, where mediation skills will remain critical.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date] <br> [Time: 3-5 min]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>